{% doc %}
  Background media for any section or block: none, an image, or a video, with an
  optional overlay.

  ## One entry point

  A section that wants a background should not have to know whether the merchant
  picked an image or a video, nor the order the layers stack in, nor that the
  overlay must sit above the media and below the content. It renders this and
  passes the settings through.

  That matters most for the thing that is easy to get wrong: the media layer has
  to be `position: absolute` inside a `position: relative` host, and the content
  has to establish a stacking context above it. Every section that hand-rolls
  this gets it right once and then breaks it the next time a dropdown inside the
  section renders behind the overlay.

  ## Ordering

      background-media          absolute, fills the host, `z-index: 0`
        ├─ image or video       the layer itself
        └─ overlay              on top of the media
      …section content…         must be `position: relative`

  The host needs `position: relative` and the content needs to sit above this.
  `.has-background-media` in `base.css` does both, so a caller adds that class
  and nothing else.

  ## Reuse

  Nothing here knows about groups. Any section can render it:

      <div class="my-section has-background-media">
        {% render 'background-media', settings: section.settings %}
        <div class="my-section__content">…</div>
      </div>

  Parameters:
    settings (object)  A settings object carrying the keys below. Passing the
                       whole object means a section adds background support by
                       adding settings, with no change to its markup.
    priority (boolean) Eager-load the image. Above-the-fold only.
    classes  (string)  Extra classes on the wrapper.

  Settings read:
    background_media    none | image | video
    image, image_mobile, image_position, image_parallax, image_parallax_speed
    video, video_url, video_poster, video_position, video_loop
    overlay_enabled, overlay_style, overlay_color, overlay_gradient,
    overlay_opacity

  @example
    {% render 'background-media', settings: block.settings %}

  @example
    {% render 'background-media', settings: section.settings, priority: true %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign media = settings.background_media | default: 'none'

  # An overlay with no media underneath is a flat tint over the section's own
  # background colour, which the colour scheme already does better. So the
  # overlay is tied to the media rather than offered independently.
  assign show_overlay = false
  if media != 'none' and settings.overlay_enabled
    assign show_overlay = true
  endif

  # Objects are tested for truthiness, strings against `''`. Neither uses
  # `!= blank`: an unset setting is nil in Shopify and Undefined in other Liquid
  # engines, and the two disagree about whether that is blank — which is how a
  # merchant who picked Video but uploaded nothing still got an empty positioned
  # wrapper laid over the content. nil is falsy in every engine.
  assign has_layer = false
  case media
    when 'image'
      if settings.image
        assign has_layer = true
      endif
    when 'video'
      assign video_url = settings.video_url | default: ''
      if settings.video or video_url != ''
        assign has_layer = true
      endif
  endcase
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Nothing selected, or selected but never filled in — a merchant who chose Video
  and has not uploaded one yet. Rendering an empty positioned wrapper would put
  a stacking context over the content for no reason.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if has_layer -%}
  <div class="background-media {{ classes }}" aria-hidden="true">
    {%- case media -%}
      {%- when 'image' -%}
        {% render 'background-image',
           image: settings.image,
           mobile_image: settings.image_mobile,
           position: settings.image_position,
           parallax: settings.image_parallax,
           speed: settings.image_parallax_speed,
           priority: priority %}

      {%- when 'video' -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The video branch has its own poster picker rather than borrowing the
          image branch's. Sharing one control meant "Image" stayed on screen
          after the merchant chose Video, which reads as a leftover rather than
          as the poster it actually was.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {% render 'background-video',
           video: settings.video,
           video_url: settings.video_url,
           image: settings.video_poster,
           position: settings.video_position,
           loop: settings.video_loop %}
    {%- endcase -%}

    {%- if show_overlay -%}
      {% render 'overlay',
         style: settings.overlay_style,
         color: settings.overlay_color,
         gradient: settings.overlay_gradient,
         opacity: settings.overlay_opacity %}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
